WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.520
When it comes to horror movies, we've seen
it all: aliens, werewolves, vampires, homicidal

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.240
clowns, and even creepy dolls.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.440
"Hahahahahahaha!

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.280
I don't believe it, I just don't believe it."

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.059
With so much shock factor already out of the
way, though, there are still some movies that

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.420
catch us off guard -- especially those with
endings that are horrifying head-scratchers.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.990
Nothing's scarier than walking away from a
movie confused, so let's dive right into our

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:29.090
nightmares and try to get some of these confusing
horror movie endings explained...

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:30.439
The Shining

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:34.390
Stanley Kubrick's iconic interpretation of
Stephen King's novel, The Shining, took a

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:38.699
lot of liberties with the source material
and turned what was an elaborate hotel story

00:00:38.699 --> 00:00:40.409
into something much more cerebral.

00:00:40.409 --> 00:00:44.659
The psychic concept of "Shining" has spawned
countless books and documentaries which have

00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:48.539
tried to make sense of the movie, but one
thing we can all agree on is that after trying

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:53.539
to murder his wife and son, Jack Nicholson's
character most definitely froze to death outside

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:55.549
of the Overlook Hotel there at the end.

00:00:55.549 --> 00:00:56.549
Yep, dead.

00:00:56.549 --> 00:01:00.210
Fans might remember the sight of Danny and
Wendy Torrance escaping in a snowmobile as

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.049
the movie ends with a close-up of a framed
photo hanging in the presumably haunted hotel.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:11.120
The picture is dated July 4, 1921, with Jack
Torrance smiling in front of a crowd of partygoers,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.770
which understandably is pretty perplexing.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:17.969
Some have theorized that Kubrick, a stickler
for details in his movies, specifically posed

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:22.390
Nicholson like this as a reference to old
18th century drawings of the devil.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.490
This would suggest that "dull boy" Jack went
to hell for trying to kill his family, regardless

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.700
of whether his rampage was caused by the ghosts
of the hotel or a particularly nasty strain

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.210
of cabin fever.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.390
Jacob's Ladder

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:38.920
The 1990 psychological thriller Jacob's Ladder
features Tim Robbins as Jacob Singer, a Vietnam

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.770
War vet who is stabbed by a bayonet on the
battlefield and returns to the States to a

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.619
whole new life, where his cozy pre-war role
as a husband and father is just a memory.

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:51.859
Before being shipped off to the battlefield,
Jacob had a wife and three sons, the youngest

00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:54.390
of whom, Gabe, died before he deployed.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.109
After returning from the war, he moves in
with new girlfriend Jezzie, but suffers from

00:01:58.109 --> 00:02:00.821
nightmarish visions of monsters and other
surreal imagery.

00:02:00.821 --> 00:02:05.670
The film bounces back and forth between Jacob's
realities -- from his future life with Jezzie

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.890
to his past family -- while he believes he’s
experiencing some kind of after-effect from

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.860
a chemical agent he was exposed to in Vietnam.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.020
Even worse, the events of the movie are revealed
to be only happening in his mind, all while

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.650
he lay dying from a stab wound on the field.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.150
Just before the triage doctors declare him
dead, Jacob mentally goes home to his apartment

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.440
before the war and reunites with Gabe, who
leads his father up their home's staircase

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.540
into a bright light.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.760
It’s all pretty complicated, but in the
end, the final scene seemed to mean that Jacob

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.530
had finally found peace in the afterlife.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.260
In the Mouth of Madness

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.650
John Carpenter's In the Mouth of Madness lived
up to its name by going off the deep end in

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:43.849
ways that would make H.P.

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:48.870
Lovecraft blush, and even outdid Inception
with its story-within-a-story metafiction

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:49.870
motif.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:54.209
In the movie, Sam Neill plays John Trent,
an insurance investigator sent to look for

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:58.720
a famous record-breaking horror author named
Sutter Cane, a moniker meant to pay homage

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.409
to author Stephen King.

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:04.239
Cane goes missing just as his next bestseller
— take a wild guess what it's called — was

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:08.459
expected to break world records, so Trent
treks off to New England to search for the

00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:11.510
author, and ends up reliving many parts of
Cane's stories.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.750
In the movie, Cane's novel is so embraced
by the masses that his stories actually unleash

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.980
a bunch of deities from another dimension,
intent on taking over the world, and Trent

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.269
discovers he’s actually just a character
in Cane's story.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.349
"I am not a piece of fiction."

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:29.330
What's even crazier is that Cane turns out
to be the portal through which these monsters

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.330
emerged.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:34.209
Neill's character eventually wakes up in a
rundown psychiatric ward and discovered the

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:38.720
world around him descending into hell, with
widespread suicides and murders as monsters

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:39.720
roamed the Earth.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.870
Through all the madness afoot, Trent finds
a movie theater playing the theatrical version

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:49.079
of Cane's book, and, in another layer of weird,
the movie replays all of his experiences,

00:03:49.079 --> 00:03:53.810
firming that he really was a Sutter Cane character
all along...and only became real as the story

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:54.810
unfolded.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.739
Did you get all that?

00:03:56.739 --> 00:03:58.410
Let the RIght One In

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:02.870
This Swedish vampire love story -- and we're
talking about the original here, not the 2010

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:08.150
remake, Let Me In -- introduces Oskar, a 12-year-old
bully victim who makes pals with a secret

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.969
blood-drinker named Eli.

00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:14.670
Oskar wants revenge against his cruel school-aged
tormentors, and Eli is all too happy to oblige.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.000
But there’s a catch.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.450
See, Eli lives with an old man named Hakan,
who tries to get in the way of Oskar's relationship

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:24.460
with Eli, because he doesn't want to be replaced
as her food source provider.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.820
Hakan is eventually caught in the act of scaring
up some lunch for the girl and splashes acid

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:33.610
on his face to keep his identity secret before
offering up his own life as a drink for Eli.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:37.540
At the end of the movie, after Eli’s done
away with the meanies in Oskar's life, the

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:42.031
two run away together, and their last scene
together shows Eli in a large box next to

00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:44.640
Oskar on a train, talking to each other through
morse code.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.470
It looks like Oskar is probably on his way
to growing old and repeating the life of the

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:52.970
late Hakan, who was probably once a little
boy who loved Eli, too… and the film ends

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:57.440
on that suggestive note, just as John Ajvide
Lindqvist's novel of the same name had.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.920
However, the movie neglects to include the
author's epilogue.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.600
Lindqvist didn't like the popular theory that
Oskar was becoming the new Hakan at the end

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:09.190
of the story and penned a subsequent short
story called "Let the Old Dreams Die" which

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:15.070
shows Eli and Oskar in a photo from 2008,
almost three decades after the events of the

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.070
story.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:19.570
The couple still appears young and in love,
implying that Oskar actually joins his girlfriend

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:20.720
in immortal undead-ness.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.040
Thanks for watching!

